Z||000004||HYPTNOTiSE||11-23-2000||04:59 AM||||  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/entireS_1.jpg">   Thats my car with 18x7.5 konig monsoons. 225/40/18 perelli's on them.  Fits great also the car is lowered 2 inches. Actually this picture shows how the 18's are shorter than 225/60/15's on stock tires. 18's on the left, 15's on the right.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/heightS.jpg">  <P>------------------<BR>       Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ Nitrous<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/hypsig.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by HYPTNOTiSE (edited 11-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Slammed Fiero||11-23-2000||11:54 AM||||Chris moore has 18" on the rear , 17" on the front of his 87 N* , He went to 17's on his convert fiero. The reason his car rides hard is that he has 425lbs rear spring rates and a tubular front end from either RCC or Ryan , either wat he said the front end of the car is not useful at all for street. The car creaks and growns a lot. The travel of the spring is not enough for the car...thus the harsh ride.<P>Jonatan McCreery<BR>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||RNGCRSH||11-24-2000||11:57 PM||||I am looking at the Zenetti Racing Snyper  <A HREF="http://www.discounttiredirect.com/" TARGET=_blank>www.discounttiredirect.com/</A> 18"x7.5" wheels they have a 42mm offset with 225 40 BF goodrich tires. Is this offset or tire width to much? or will it work? I plan on having RCC coilover setup on all 4 points with tubular arms and corvette brakes. <p>[This message has been edited by RNGCRSH (edited 11-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfiero||11-25-2000||01:53 AM||||OOH baby!  I love the looks of those racing snypers too!!  A little on the pricey side, but VERY nice looking.  <P>You shouldn't have any problems with the tire width or offset......a 42 mm offset works well on the Fiero (from what I've ben told).   ||||reg||10
